1
00:00:01,537 --> 00:00:06,687
SeatLock demo: seat locking, confirmation, cancellation, expiry, conflicts and 100 concurrent users.

2
00:00:06,687 --> 00:00:11,604
The React client loads live seat status from the Spring Boot API backed by PostgreSQL.

3
00:00:11,604 --> 00:00:16,376
One coach with 20 seats. Each seat is Available, Held (locked) or Booked.

4
00:00:16,376 --> 00:00:18,698
Select seats 1A and 1B, then enter passenger details.

5
00:00:18,698 --> 00:00:23,700
The seats are now locked in the database with a countdown timer (shortened to 20 s for this recording; default is 2 minutes).

6
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:26,667
Simulated payment: Confirm & pay turns the hold into a durable booking.

7
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:32,523
Booking confirmed with a unique reference. Seats 1A and 1B are now shown as Booked.

8
00:00:32,523 --> 00:00:38,191
Now hold seat 2A and cancel the reservation.

9
00:00:38,191 --> 00:00:42,695
Cancelling releases the lock immediately: seat 2A is Available again.

10
00:00:42,695 --> 00:00:47,250
Hold seat 3A and let the lock expire without confirming.

11
00:00:47,250 --> 00:01:05,225
Waiting for the countdown to reach zero. Expired locks can never be confirmed.

12
00:01:05,225 --> 00:01:10,229
The hold expired and seat 3A became Available again. The server rejects late confirmations with 410 Gone.

13
00:01:10,229 --> 00:01:13,418
Conflict: we select seat 4A while another customer grabs it first through the API.

14
00:01:13,418 --> 00:01:19,478
The API answers 409 SEAT_UNAVAILABLE and the UI shows a clear error. No seat is ever double-booked.

15
00:01:19,478 --> 00:01:24,589
Core challenge: a script releases 100 customers at the same moment, five competing for each of the 20 seats.

16
00:01:24,589 --> 00:01:32,594
Result: only the free seats were confirmed, the rest got 409, and the database holds zero duplicate seats.

17
00:01:32,594 --> 00:01:38,002
Back in the UI every seat is Booked — never more than 20 confirmations.

18
00:01:38,002 --> 00:01:42,544
Booking history lists each confirmed reference; every seat appears exactly once.

19
00:01:42,544 --> 00:01:48,546
Row locks plus a database UNIQUE constraint guarantee one passenger per seat. Thanks for watching!
